  "ablation_size_label": "scored case: 100,000,000 floats (ranking_by LAST)",
  "takeaways": [
    "**Exact match, so radix:** the output must equal torch.sort; a radix sort on order-preserving key bits is exact by construction. One min/max pass lets every pass sort key - kmin: the scored data differ only in their low 24 bits, so 3 passes instead of 4, correct for any input.",
    "**The H100 #1 (129.7 us) is below the memory floor** (~240 us to read and write 800 MB): the leaders exploit the input's near-sortedness. The median is 2094 us; Crisp ranks 19/30 at 3749.5 us.",
    "**The downsweep is instruction-bound, not memory-bound:** probes showed ~38K cycles per 4096-key tile; packed register counters bought nothing, a run-time ash cost ~200 us per pass. The next lever is a warp ballot/match builtin for one-pass 8-bit ranking (a compiler feature).",
    "**Onesweep (decoupled look-back) was correct but slower** here (1330 vs 1236 us per pass).",
    "**Two compiler bugs fixed on the way:** BUG 138 (ash/popcount were out-of-line calls on PTX) and BUG 145 (a while ending a let crashed the analyzer). Endeavour 200: tests/spec/200-sort-contest/sort-contest.md."
  ],
  "beauty_entry": {
    "crisp_file": "07-sort-v2/sort_bitonic_beauty.crisp",
